WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.679
Single-machine planning is solved.

00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:11.357
Now Listens is one hundred terabytes spread across one thousand machines.

00:00:11.357 --> 00:00:16.036
No one node holds enough to join it to Users alone.

00:00:16.036 --> 00:00:20.715
One thousand nodes can run it roughly one hundred times faster, but only if the optimizer can get matching keys onto the same machine first.

00:00:20.715 --> 00:00:25.484
Watch the central operation.

00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:30.253
Start with the rows scattered across three nodes.

00:00:30.253 --> 00:00:35.022
User forty-two sits on Node A, but copies of seven and thirteen are strewn everywhere, each key wherever it happened to land.

00:00:35.022 --> 00:00:39.791
No node can join yet.

00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:44.560
So shuffle.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.329
Send each row to hash of the key mod the node count, a target machine picked by the key, over the network.

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:54.098
After the shuffle, every row with the same key on the same node.

00:00:54.098 --> 00:00:58.867
All of user forty-two on Node A, all of seven on Node B, all of thirteen on Node C.

00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:03.636
Now each node runs an ordinary local join on the rows it owns.

00:01:03.636 --> 00:01:08.405
This is exactly hash partitioning, just with the network as the destination.

00:01:08.405 --> 00:01:13.174
Local hash partitioning routes by hash mod B to a file on the same disk.

00:01:13.174 --> 00:01:17.943
Shuffle routes by hash mod nodes to another machine.

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:22.712
Same two NI Os, plus the network transfer, and the network transfer is the dominant term.

00:01:22.712 --> 00:01:26.209
You do not always shuffle both sides.

00:01:26.209 --> 00:01:29.706
Broadcast shuffle sends one copy of the entire small table to every node.

00:01:29.706 --> 00:01:33.203
Cost is the small table size times the node count.

00:01:33.203 --> 00:01:36.700
Best when a tiny table joins a massive one.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.197
Hash shuffle redistributes both tables by a common key.

00:01:40.197 --> 00:01:43.693
Cost is the total size of both.

00:01:43.693 --> 00:01:47.190
Best when both are large.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.687
Co-located shuffle moves nothing at all.

00:01:50.687 --> 00:01:54.184
The data is already partitioned on the join key, so the cost is zero.

00:01:54.184 --> 00:01:57.681
Best when the tables were laid out together up front.

00:01:57.681 --> 00:02:01.797
Aggregation gets the same treatment.

00:02:01.797 --> 00:02:05.912
A global GROUP BY across one thousand nodes would flood the network with billions of tiny rows.

00:02:05.912 --> 00:02:10.028
So split it.

00:02:10.028 --> 00:02:14.144
Phase one, each node pre-aggregates its own data.

00:02:14.144 --> 00:02:18.260
One million listen rows per node collapse to one thousand artist totals.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.375
The data shrinks a thousandfold before the network sees any of it.

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:26.491
Phase two, those partials shuffle by artist to a coordinator, which sums them into the true totals.

00:02:26.491 --> 00:02:30.607
Same answer, a fraction of the load.

00:02:30.607 --> 00:02:35.739
This is how the real systems work.

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:40.870
Spark holds data in memory as RD Ds and rewrites the plan with its Catalyst optimizer, picking hash and broadcast joins.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:46.002
BigQuery runs a tree-shaped execution where each level aggregates partials from the level below.

00:02:46.002 --> 00:02:51.134
Snowflake separates storage from compute over cached micro-partitions.

00:02:51.134 --> 00:02:56.265
Presto and Trino pipeline operators across nodes and filter dynamically.

00:02:56.265 --> 00:03:01.397
Different systems, the same core ideas.

00:03:01.397 --> 00:03:05.398
Distributed planning is single-machine cost reasoning stretched across the network.

00:03:05.398 --> 00:03:09.400
Shuffle is hash partitioning over the wire.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.401
Broadcast for small times big, hash shuffle for big times big, co-located for free.

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:17.403
Two-phase aggregation shrinks the data before it ships.

00:03:17.403 --> 00:03:21.404
Next we tie the whole course together.

00:03:21.404 --> 00:03:25.406
One click on a play button, three systems, eight algorithms, two seconds.
